On 2009-10-19, Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> Ilmari Karonen wrote:
>> 
>> Anyway, I did think of a way to make fake amulets of Yendor a little
>> bit more interesting some time ago: make any newly generated fake
>> amulets have the same #name as the real amulet currently has.
>>
>> So, yes, you can still name the real AoY "REAL", but the next time
>> Rodney steals it and double-troubles, the fake the clone is carrying
>> will _also_ be named "REAL".  Of course, that still leaves several
>> easy ways to tell the real and fake amulets apart, but at least you'd
>> have to spend a _little_ effort on it.
>
> Mind that;
> "whether you put the Amulet of Yendor in inventory slot 'Y' or
>   name it "real" doesn't really make any difference in practice".
>
> Would you want to change that game mechanics (same slot) as well?
> Just to obscure the Amulet identity after it had been stolen?

Why not?  Actually, it would seem to me quite reasonable to give newly
created fake amulets *all* the modifiable attributes the real one
currently has.  So if the real AoY happens to be blessed, greased,
named "REAL" and assigned to slot Y, any new fakes should be so also.

At least for the fakes carried by Rodney's duplicates, this would seem
to make in-game sense: they're created as part of a magical cloning
process, and so could be assumed to be copies of the real amulet.  Not
to mention that, in any case, one really would _expect_ him to try to
make them as similar to the real amulet as possible.

(Anyway, inventory slots are much easier to change accidentally than
object names.  They may stay the same most of the time, but I'd really
rather prefer to double-check than to rely on them alone to tell which
amulet is the real one.)

